.01308441162109375, 0.03424072265625, 0.04010009765625, 0.019134521484375, 0.0027561187744140625, 0.0253143310546875, 0.0159149169921875, -0.043731689453125, -0.0012826919555664062, -0.00909423828125, 0.0238037109375, -0.00475311279296875, -0.0193023681640625, 0.0175628662109375, -0.01690673828125, -0.035614013671875, 0.031982421875, 0.0032806396484375, -0.0004088878631591797, 0.00717926025390625, -0.01076507568359375, -0.0159759521484375, 0.0200958251953125, -0.0116424560546875, -0.0258331298828125, 0.0310211181640625, 0.0025691986083984375, 0.0300445556640625, 0.0065460205078125, -0.0294647216796875, -0.01395416259765625, 0.00577545166015625, 0.019439697265625, 0.0019178390502929688, -0.00231170654296875, 0.008575439453125, -0.0247802734375, 0.0197906494140625, -0.0238800048828125, -0.00371551513671875, 0.042266845703125, -0.0018091201782226562, 0.006725311279296875, 0.03399658203125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent research has explored the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, particularly in electrical engineering and physics. GA provides a unified mathematical language for representing geometric transformations and relationships, often simplifying the analysis of multidimensional systems and offering new insights into their structure compared to traditional vector or matrix approaches. Many problems in electromagnetics, robotics, and control theory involve quantities such as rotations, bivectors, or coupled vector fields that are naturally and compactly represented as multivector-valued functions. For example, in the analysis of three-phase electrical machines, the state variables are best described as elements of a Clifford algebra, and in rigid body dynamics, the orientation and angular velocity are naturally encoded as rotors and bivectors, respectively. In such cases, the classical Laplace transform, which acts on scalar- or vector-valued functions, is inadequate for directly analyzing systems where the unknowns or inputs are multivectors. Extending the Laplace transform to multivector-valued functions enables direct application of powerful integral transform techniques to these problems, facilitating solution, stability analysis, and control design in a manner that respects the underlying geometric structure.\n\n**Roadmap and Structure**  \nThis document is organized as follows. We first introduce the necessary definitions and notation for geometric algebras and multivector-valued functions. Next, we discuss the function spaces relevant for the Laplace transform and the existence of the transform. We then define the Laplace transform in the GA setting, justify the restriction to scalar Laplace parameters, and systematically analyze the mathematical obstacles to more general definitions. A concrete example is provided to illustrate the application of the proposed transform. We then review and critically compare related work, discuss inversion and uniqueness, and address computational aspects. Finally, we clarify the scope and dimensionality of our results.\n\n**Definitions and Notation**  \nLet \\(\\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\) denote the real geometric (Clifford) algebra of signature \\((p,q)\\), generated by an orthonormal basis \\(\\{e_1, \\ldots, e_{p+q}\\}\\) with \\(e_i^2 = 1\\) for \\(1 \\leq i \\leq p\\) and \\(e_i^2 = -1\\) for \\(p < i \\leq p+q\\). A *multivector* in \\(\\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\) is a linear combination of basis blades (elements of different grades). A *multivector-valued function* is a function \\(f: [0, \\infty) \\to \\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\), i.e., for each \\(t \\geq 0\\), \\(f(t)\\) is a multivector. The *exponential map* for a multivector \\(A\\) is defined by the power series \\(e^{A} = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\frac{A^k}{k!}\\), but closed forms are generally available only for special cases (e.g., blades or bivectors).\n\nWe use the Euclidean (Hilbert-Schmidt) norm for multivectors, defined as \\(\\|f(t)\\| = \\left( \\sum_A |f_A(t)|^2 \\right)^{1/2}\\), where \\(f(t) = \\sum_A f_A(t) A\\) is the expansion in the basis blades \\(A\\). This norm is standard in the literature, is submultiplicative, and ensures that convergence and integrability properties are well-defined for the component functions.\n\n**Function Spaces and Existence**  \nWe define the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions \\(f: [0, \\infty) \\to \\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\) as follows. Let \\(L^1([0, \\infty), \\mathcal{G}(p,q))\\) denote the space of functions for which each real coefficient function (in the basis expansion) is Lebesgue integrable on \\([0, \\infty)\\). That is, if \\(f(t) = \\sum_{A} f_A(t) A\\) (sum over basis blades \\(A\\)), then \\(f_A \\in L^1([0, \\infty), \\mathbb{R})\\) for all \\(A\\). This choice ensures that the Laplace integral converges for each component. Alternative function spaces, such as \\(L^2([0, \\infty), \\mathcal{G}(p,q))\\) (square-integrable functions), or spaces of continuous or differentiable multivector-valued functions, may also be considered depending on the application. However, \\(L^1\\) is the minimal requirement for the existence of the Laplace transform as an improper integral. For functions with faster than exponential growth, the Laplace transform may not exist; for functions in \\(L^2\\) or smoother spaces, additional properties such as uniqueness and inversion may be established, but these are not guaranteed in the general \\(L^1\\) setting. Thus, our definition is applicable to functions whose norm grows at most exponentially, i.e., there exist constants \\(M, \\alpha\\) such that \\(\\|f(t)\\| \\leq M e^{\\alpha t}\\).\n\n**Definition of the Laplace Transform in GA**  \nFor \\(f \\in L^1([0, \\infty), \\mathcal{G}(p,q))\\) and Laplace parameter \\(s \\in \\mathbb{C}\\) with \\(\\operatorname{Re}(s) > s_0\\) (for some \\(s_0\\) depending on the growth of \\(f\\)), we define\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}[f](s) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\, dt,\n\\]\nwhere \\(e^{-st}\\) is the standard scalar exponential, which commutes with all multivectors in the integrand. This ensures that the integral is well-defined, linear, and that the Laplace transform retains classical properties such as linearity and shift invariance. The transform exists and is unique for all \\(f\\) such that \\(\\|f(t)\\|\\) grows at most exponentially as \\(t \\to \\infty\\).\n\n**Justification for Scalar Restriction and Systematic Analysis of Obstacles**  \nAllowing \\(s\\) to be a general multivector leads to significant mathematical obstacles. We formalize these as follows:\n\n1. **Non-commutativity and Ambiguity:** For general \\(s \\in \\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\), the exponential \\(e^{-st}\\) does not, in general, commute with \\(f(t)\\). Thus, the expressions \\(e^{-st} f(t)\\) and \\(f(t) e^{-st}\\) can differ, as shown by the following calculation in \\(\\mathcal{G}(2,0)\\): let \\(s = e_1\\), \\(f(t) = e_2\\). Then,\n   \\[\n   e^{-e_1 t} e_2 = (\\cosh t - e_1 \\sinh t) e_2 = e_2 \\cosh t - (e_1 e_2) \\sinh t,\n   \\]\n   while\n   \\[\n   e_2 e^{-e_1 t} = e_2 (\\cosh t - e_1 \\sinh t) = e_2 \\cosh t - (e_2 e_1) \\sinh t.\n   \\]\n   Since \\(e_1 e_2 = -e_2 e_1\\), the two expressions differ by a sign in the bivector term, demonstrating non-commutativity and ambiguity in the definition.\n\n2. **Lack of Closed Forms and Convergence:** The exponential map \\(e^{-st}\\) is only guaranteed to converge and have a closed form when \\(s\\) is a scalar or, in special cases, a blade or bivector (see Ablamowicz & Fauser, *Clifford Algebras and their Applications in Mathematical Physics*, 2004). For general multivectors, the power series may not converge or may not yield a tractable expression, especially in higher dimensions.\n\n3. **Breakdown of Classical Properties:** The Laplace transform\u2019s classical properties\u2014such as linearity, shift invariance, and the convolution theorem\u2014rely on the commutativity and associativity of the scalar exponential with the function being transformed. For non-scalar \\(s\\), these properties can fail, as the order of multiplication affects the result and associativity may not hold for products involving exponentials of non-commuting multivectors.\n\n4. **Invertibility and Uniqueness:** The invertibility of the Laplace transform depends on the invertibility of the exponential map. For arbitrary multivectors, \\(e^{-st}\\) may not be invertible, undermining the construction of inverse transforms and the solution of algebraic equations in the Laplace domain.\n\n5. **Computational Intractability:** In higher dimensions, the number of basis elements grows exponentially, and the lack of closed forms for the exponential map makes explicit computation infeasible.\n\nTherefore, we restrict \\(s\\) to complex scalars to ensure mathematical consistency and to preserve the essential properties of the Laplace transform.\n\n**Concrete Example: Application to a Simple Multivector Differential Equation**  \nConsider the first-order differential equation in \\(\\mathcal{G}(2,0)\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dt} f(t) + \\omega e_{12} f(t) = g(t),\n\\]\nwhere \\(e_{12} = e_1 e_2\\) is the unit bivector,"}}